{"surah": 49, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ لَا تُقَدِّمُوا۟ بَيْنَ يَدَىِ ٱللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِۦۖ وَٱتَّقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ سَمِيعٌ عَلِيمٌ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, do not put [yourselves] before Allāh and His Messenger[1] but fear Allāh. Indeed, Allāh is Hearing and Knowing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تُقَدِّمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmِّdَqُt", "root": "قدم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَدَىِ", "translit": "ِàَdَy", "root": "يدي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَرَسُولِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlwُsَrَw", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱتَّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَّtٱَw", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "سَمِيعٌ", "translit": "ٌʿعyِmَs", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ لَا تَرْفَعُوٓا۟ أَصْوَٰتَكُمْ فَوْقَ صَوْتِ ٱلنَّبِىِّ وَلَا تَجْهَرُوا۟ لَهُۥ بِٱلْقَوْلِ كَجَهْرِ بَعْضِكُمْ لِبَعْضٍ أَن تَحْبَطَ أَعْمَٰلُكُمْ وَأَنتُمْ لَا تَشْعُرُونَ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet or be loud to him in speech like the loudness of some of you to others, lest your deeds become worthless while you perceive not.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَرْفَعُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُʿعَfْrَt", "root": "رفع"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَصْوَٰتَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَtَٰwْṣَaʾ", "root": "صوت"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَوْقَ", "translit": "َqْwَf", "root": "فوق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "صَوْتِ", "translit": "ِtْwَṣ", "root": "صوت"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلنَّبِىِّ", "translit": "ِّàِbَّnlٱ", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَجْهَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَhْjgَt", "root": "جهر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بِٱلْقَوْلِ", "translit": "ِlْwَqْlٱِb", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَجَهْرِ", "translit": "ِrْhَjgَk", "root": "جهر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "بَعْضِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لِبَعْضٍ", "translit": "ٍḍْʿعَbِl", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "تَحْبَطَ", "translit": "َṭَbْḥَt", "root": "حبط"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَأَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "تَشْعُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُʿعْhsَt", "root": "شعر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ يَغُضُّونَ أَصْوَٰتَهُمْ عِندَ رَسُولِ ٱللَّهِ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ ٱمْتَحَنَ ٱللَّهُ قُلُوبَهُمْ لِلتَّقْوَىٰۚ لَهُم مَّغْفِرَةٌ وَأَجْرٌ عَظِيمٌ ", "translation": "Indeed, those who lower their voices before the Messenger of Allāh - they are the ones whose hearts Allāh has tested for righteousness. For them is forgiveness and great reward.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَغُضُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّḍُغَy", "root": "غضض"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَصْوَٰتَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَtَٰwْṣَaʾ", "root": "صوت"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَسُولِ", "translit": "ِlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱمْتَحَنَ", "translit": "َnَḥَtْmٱ", "root": "محن"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "قُلُوبَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لِلتَّقْوَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَwْqَّtlِl", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مَّغْفِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِfْغَّm", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَأَجْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْjgَaʾَw", "root": "اجر"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "عَظِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِẓَʿع", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ يُنَادُونَكَ مِن وَرَآءِ ٱلْحُجُرَٰتِ أَكْثَرُهُمْ لَا يَعْقِلُونَ ", "translation": "Indeed, those who call you, [O Muḥammad], from behind the chambers - most of them do not use reason.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُنَادُونَكَ", "translit": "َkَnwُdāَnُy", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَرَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَrَw", "root": "وري"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْحُجُرَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰrُjgُḥْlٱ", "root": "حجر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَكْثَرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُrَhtْkَaʾ", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَعْقِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِqْʿعَy", "root": "عقل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "وَلَوْ أَنَّهُمْ صَبَرُوا۟ حَتَّىٰ تَخْرُجَ إِلَيْهِمْ لَكَانَ خَيْرًا لَّهُمْۚ وَٱللَّهُ غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ ", "translation": "And if they had been patient until you [could] come out to them, it would have been better for them. But Allāh is Forgiving and Merciful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَوْ", "translit": "ْwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "صَبَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَbَṣ", "root": "صبر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَخْرُجَ", "translit": "َjgُrْhkَt", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَl", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "خَيْرًا", "translit": "āًrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "غَفُورٌ", "translit": "ٌrwُfَغ", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "رَّحِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِḥَّr", "root": "رحم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوٓا۟ إِن جَآءَكُمْ فَاسِقٌۢ بِنَبَإٍ فَتَبَيَّنُوٓا۟ أَن تُصِيبُوا۟ قَوْمًۢا بِجَهَٰلَةٍ فَتُصْبِحُوا۟ عَلَىٰ مَا فَعَلْتُمْ نَٰدِمِينَ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, if there comes to you a disobedient one with information, investigate, lest you harm a people out of ignorance and become, over what you have done, regretful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "جَآءَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَاسِقٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢqِsāَf", "root": "فسق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِنَبَإٍ", "translit": "ٍiʾَbَnِb", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَتَبَيَّنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnَّyَbَtَf", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تُصِيبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbyِṣُt", "root": "صوب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قَوْمًۢا", "translit": "āًۢmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِجَهَٰلَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَlَٰhَjgِb", "root": "جهل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَتُصْبِحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥِbْṣُtَf", "root": "صبح"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَعَلْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْlَʿعَf", "root": "فعل"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "نَٰدِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِdَٰn", "root": "ندم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "وَٱعْلَمُوٓا۟ أَنَّ فِيكُمْ رَسُولَ ٱللَّهِۚ لَوْ يُطِيعُكُمْ فِى كَثِيرٍ مِّنَ ٱلْأَمْرِ لَعَنِتُّمْ وَلَٰكِنَّ ٱللَّهَ حَبَّبَ إِلَيْكُمُ ٱلْإِيمَٰنَ وَزَيَّنَهُۥ فِى قُلُوبِكُمْ وَكَرَّهَ إِلَيْكُمُ ٱلْكُفْرَ وَٱلْفُسُوقَ وَٱلْعِصْيَانَۚ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلرَّٰشِدُونَ ", "translation": "And know that among you is the Messenger of Allāh. If he were to obey you in much of the matter, you would be in difficulty, but Allāh has endeared to you the faith and has made it pleasing in your hearts and has made hateful to you disbelief, defiance and disobedience. Those are the [rightly] guided.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱعْلَمُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُmَlْʿعٱَw", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِيكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَسُولَ", "translit": "َlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَوْ", "translit": "ْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يُطِيعُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُʿعyِṭُy", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَثِيرٍ", "translit": "ٍryِhtَk", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْرِ", "translit": "ِrْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَعَنِتُّمْ", "translit": "ْmُّtِnَʿعَl", "root": "عنت"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِkَٰlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "حَبَّبَ", "translit": "َbَّbَḥ", "root": "حبب"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "إِلَيْكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْإِيمَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰmyِiʾْlٱ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَزَيَّنَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnَّyَzَw", "root": "زين"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "قُلُوبِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَكَرَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّrَkَw", "root": "كره"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "إِلَيْكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلْكُفْرَ", "translit": "َrْfُkْlٱ", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "وَٱلْفُسُوقَ", "translit": "َqwُsُfْlٱَw", "root": "فسق"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "وَٱلْعِصْيَانَ", "translit": "َnāَyْṣِʿعْlٱَw", "root": "عصي"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "ٱلرَّٰشِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِhsَّٰrlٱ", "root": "رشد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "فَضْلًا مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ وَنِعْمَةًۚ وَٱللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ حَكِيمٌ ", "translation": "[It is] as bounty from Allāh and favor. And Allāh is Knowing and Wise.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَضْلًا", "translit": "āًlْḍَf", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَنِعْمَةً", "translit": "ًẗَmْʿعِnَw", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "حَكِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِkَḥ", "root": "حكم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "وَإِن طَآئِفَتَانِ مِنَ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ ٱقْتَتَلُوا۟ فَأَصْلِحُوا۟ بَيْنَهُمَاۖ فَإِنۢ بَغَتْ إِحْدَىٰهُمَا عَلَى ٱلْأُخْرَىٰ فَقَٰتِلُوا۟ ٱلَّتِى تَبْغِى حَتَّىٰ تَفِىٓءَ إِلَىٰٓ أَمْرِ ٱللَّهِۚ فَإِن فَآءَتْ فَأَصْلِحُوا۟ بَيْنَهُمَا بِٱلْعَدْلِ وَأَقْسِطُوٓا۟ۖ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يُحِبُّ ٱلْمُقْسِطِينَ ", "translation": "And if two factions among the believers should fight, then make settlement between the two. But if one of them oppresses the other, then fight against the one that oppresses until it returns to the ordinance of Allāh. And if it returns, then make settlement between them in justice and act justly. Indeed, Allāh loves those who act justly.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "طَآئِفَتَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَtَfِyʾٓāَṭ", "root": "طوف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱقْتَتَلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlَtَtْqٱ", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَأَصْلِحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥِlْṣَaʾَf", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَإِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بَغَتْ", "translit": "ْtَغَb", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِحْدَىٰهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhٰàَdْḥِiʾ", "root": "احد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْأُخْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْhkُaʾْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَقَٰتِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِtَٰqَf", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "تَبْغِى", "translit": "àِغْbَt", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "تَفِىٓءَ", "translit": "َءٓàِfَt", "root": "فيا"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "إِلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَمْرِ", "translit": "ِrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "فَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "فَآءَتْ", "translit": "ْtَءٓāَf", "root": "فيا"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "فَأَصْلِحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥِlْṣَaʾَf", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "بِٱلْعَدْلِ", "translit": "ِlْdَʿعْlٱِb", "root": "عدل"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "وَأَقْسِطُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُṭِsْqَaʾَw", "root": "قسط"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "يُحِبُّ", "translit": "ُّbِḥُy", "root": "حبب"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "ٱلْمُقْسِطِينَ", "translit": "َnyِṭِsْqُmْlٱ", "root": "قسط"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "إِنَّمَا ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ إِخْوَةٌ فَأَصْلِحُوا۟ بَيْنَ أَخَوَيْكُمْۚ وَٱتَّقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُرْحَمُونَ ", "translation": "The believers are but brothers, so make settlement between your brothers. And fear Allāh that you may receive mercy.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِخْوَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَwْhkِiʾ", "root": "اخو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَأَصْلِحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥِlْṣَaʾَf", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَخَوَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَwَhkَaʾ", "root": "اخو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱتَّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَّtٱَw", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَعَلَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّlَʿعَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تُرْحَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَḥْrُt", "root": "رحم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ لَا يَسْخَرْ قَوْمٌ مِّن قَوْمٍ عَسَىٰٓ أَن يَكُونُوا۟ خَيْرًا مِّنْهُمْ وَلَا نِسَآءٌ مِّن نِّسَآءٍ عَسَىٰٓ أَن يَكُنَّ خَيْرًا مِّنْهُنَّۖ وَلَا تَلْمِزُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَكُمْ وَلَا تَنَابَزُوا۟ بِٱلْأَلْقَٰبِۖ بِئْسَ ٱلِٱسْمُ ٱلْفُسُوقُ بَعْدَ ٱلْإِيمَٰنِۚ وَمَن لَّمْ يَتُبْ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, let not a people ridicule [another] people; perhaps they may be better than them; nor let women ridicule [other] women; perhaps they may be better than them. And do not insult one another and do not call each other by [offensive] nicknames. Wretched is the name [i.e., mention] of disobedience after [one's] faith. And whoever does not repent - then it is those who are the wrongdoers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَسْخَرْ", "translit": "ْrَhkْsَy", "root": "سخر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَوْمٌ", "translit": "ٌmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَسَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَsَʿع", "root": "عسي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَكُونُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnwُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "خَيْرًا", "translit": "āًrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِّنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "نِسَآءٌ", "translit": "ٌءٓāَsِn", "root": "نسو"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "نِّسَآءٍ", "translit": "ٍءٓāَsِّn", "root": "نسو"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "عَسَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَsَʿع", "root": "عسي"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "يَكُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "خَيْرًا", "translit": "āًrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "مِّنْهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "تَلْمِزُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُzِmْlَt", "root": "لمز"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "أَنفُسَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَsُfnَaʾ", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "تَنَابَزُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُzَbāَnَt", "root": "نبز"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "بِٱلْأَلْقَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰqْlَaʾْlٱِb", "root": "لقب"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "بِئْسَ", "translit": "َsْyʾِb", "root": "باس"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "ٱلِٱسْمُ", "translit": "ُmْsٱِlٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "ٱلْفُسُوقُ", "translit": "ُqwُsُfْlٱ", "root": "فسق"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "بَعْدَ", "translit": "َdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "ٱلْإِيمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmyِiʾْlٱ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "لَّمْ", "translit": "ْmَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "يَتُبْ", "translit": "ْbُtَy", "root": "توب"}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ ٱجْتَنِبُوا۟ كَثِيرًا مِّنَ ٱلظَّنِّ إِنَّ بَعْضَ ٱلظَّنِّ إِثْمٌۖ وَلَا تَجَسَّسُوا۟ وَلَا يَغْتَب بَّعْضُكُم بَعْضًاۚ أَيُحِبُّ أَحَدُكُمْ أَن يَأْكُلَ لَحْمَ أَخِيهِ مَيْتًا فَكَرِهْتُمُوهُۚ وَٱتَّقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ تَوَّابٌ رَّحِيمٌ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, avoid much [negative] assumption. Indeed, some assumption is sin. And do not spy or backbite each other. Would one of you like to eat the flesh of his brother when dead? You would detest it. And fear Allāh; indeed, Allāh is Accepting of Repentance and Merciful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱجْتَنِبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbِnَtْjgٱ", "root": "جنب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَثِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِhtَk", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلظَّنِّ", "translit": "ِّnَّẓlٱ", "root": "ظنن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بَعْضَ", "translit": "َḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلظَّنِّ", "translit": "ِّnَّẓlٱ", "root": "ظنن"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِثْمٌ", "translit": "ٌmْhtِiʾ", "root": "اثم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَجَسَّسُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُsَّsَjgَt", "root": "جسس"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَغْتَب", "translit": "bَtْغَy", "root": "غيب"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بَّعْضُكُم", "translit": "mُkُḍْʿعَّb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بَعْضًا", "translit": "āًḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "أَيُحِبُّ", "translit": "ُّbِḥُyَaʾ", "root": "حبب"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَحَدُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُdَḥَaʾ", "root": "احد"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "يَأْكُلَ", "translit": "َlُkْaʾَy", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "لَحْمَ", "translit": "َmْḥَl", "root": "لحم"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "أَخِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِhkَaʾ", "root": "اخو"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "مَيْتًا", "translit": "āًtْyَm", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "فَكَرِهْتُمُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُmُtْhِrَkَf", "root": "كره"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "وَٱتَّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَّtٱَw", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "تَوَّابٌ", "translit": "ٌbāَّwَt", "root": "توب"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "رَّحِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِḥَّr", "root": "رحم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّاسُ إِنَّا خَلَقْنَٰكُم مِّن ذَكَرٍ وَأُنثَىٰ وَجَعَلْنَٰكُمْ شُعُوبًا وَقَبَآئِلَ لِتَعَارَفُوٓا۟ۚ إِنَّ أَكْرَمَكُمْ عِندَ ٱللَّهِ أَتْقَىٰكُمْۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ عَلِيمٌ خَبِيرٌ ", "translation": "O mankind, indeed We have created you from male and female and made you peoples and tribes that you may know one another. Indeed, the most noble of you in the sight of Allāh is the most righteous[1] of you. Indeed, Allāh is Knowing and Aware.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسُ", "translit": "ُsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "خَلَقْنَٰكُم", "translit": "mُkَٰnْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ذَكَرٍ", "translit": "ٍrَkَhd", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَأُنثَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhtnُaʾَw", "root": "انث"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَٰnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "شُعُوبًا", "translit": "āًbwُʿعُhs", "root": "شعب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَقَبَآئِلَ", "translit": "َlِyʾٓāَbَqَw", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لِتَعَارَفُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُfَrāَʿعَtِl", "root": "عرف"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَكْرَمَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَmَrْkَaʾ", "root": "كرم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَتْقَىٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkٰàَqْtَaʾ", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "خَبِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِbَhk", "root": "خبر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "قَالَتِ ٱلْأَعْرَابُ ءَامَنَّاۖ قُل لَّمْ تُؤْمِنُوا۟ وَلَٰكِن قُولُوٓا۟ أَسْلَمْنَا وَلَمَّا يَدْخُلِ ٱلْإِيمَٰنُ فِى قُلُوبِكُمْۖ وَإِن تُطِيعُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَرَسُولَهُۥ لَا يَلِتْكُم مِّنْ أَعْمَٰلِكُمْ شَيْـًٔاۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ ", "translation": "The bedouins say, \"We have believed.\" Say, \"You have not [yet] believed; but say [instead], 'We have submitted,' for faith has not yet entered your hearts. And if you obey Allāh and His Messenger, He will not deprive you from your deeds[1] of anything. Indeed, Allāh is Forgiving and Merciful.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَتِ", "translit": "ِtَlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْأَعْرَابُ", "translit": "ُbāَrْʿعَaʾْlٱ", "root": "عرب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قُل", "translit": "lُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَّمْ", "translit": "ْmَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تُؤْمِنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnِmْwʾُt", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِن", "translit": "nِkَٰlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قُولُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlwُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَسْلَمْنَا", "translit": "āَnْmَlْsَaʾ", "root": "سلم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَدْخُلِ", "translit": "ِlُhkْdَy", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْإِيمَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰmyِiʾْlٱ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "قُلُوبِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "تُطِيعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعyِṭُt", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَرَسُولَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlwُsَrَw", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "يَلِتْكُم", "translit": "mُkْtِlَy", "root": "ليت"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "غَفُورٌ", "translit": "ٌrwُfَغ", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "رَّحِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِḥَّr", "root": "رحم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "إِنَّمَا ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ بِٱللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِۦ ثُمَّ لَمْ يَرْتَابُوا۟ وَجَٰهَدُوا۟ بِأَمْوَٰلِهِمْ وَأَنفُسِهِمْ فِى سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِۚ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلصَّٰدِقُونَ ", "translation": "The believers are only the ones who have believed in Allāh and His Messenger and then doubt not but strive with their properties and their lives in the cause of Allāh. It is those who are the truthful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَرَسُولِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlwُsَrَw", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَرْتَابُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbāَtْrَy", "root": "ريب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَجَٰهَدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdَhَٰjgَw", "root": "جهد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بِأَمْوَٰلِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlَٰwْmَaʾِb", "root": "مول"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَأَنفُسِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِsُfnَaʾَw", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰدِقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqِdَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صدق"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "قُلْ أَتُعَلِّمُونَ ٱللَّهَ بِدِينِكُمْ وَٱللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَمَا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِۚ وَٱللَّهُ بِكُلِّ شَىْءٍ عَلِيمٌ ", "translation": "Say, \"Would you acquaint Allāh with your religion while Allāh knows whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth, and Allāh is Knowing of all things?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَتُعَلِّمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِّlَʿعُtَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِدِينِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِnyِdِb", "root": "دين"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِb", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "يَمُنُّونَ عَلَيْكَ أَنْ أَسْلَمُوا۟ۖ قُل لَّا تَمُنُّوا۟ عَلَىَّ إِسْلَٰمَكُمۖ بَلِ ٱللَّهُ يَمُنُّ عَلَيْكُمْ أَنْ هَدَىٰكُمْ لِلْإِيمَٰنِ إِن كُنتُمْ صَٰدِقِينَ ", "translation": "They consider it a favor to you that they have accepted Islām. Say, \"Do not consider your Islām a favor to me. Rather, Allāh has conferred favor upon you that He has guided you to the faith, if you should be truthful.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَمُنُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّnُmَy", "root": "منن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنْ", "translit": "ْnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَسْلَمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmَlْsَaʾ", "root": "سلم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قُل", "translit": "lُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تَمُنُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّnُmَt", "root": "منن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِسْلَٰمَكُم", "translit": "mُkَmَٰlْsِiʾ", "root": "سلم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بَلِ", "translit": "ِlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَمُنُّ", "translit": "ُّnُmَy", "root": "منن"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَنْ", "translit": "ْnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "هَدَىٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkٰàَdَh", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لِلْإِيمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmyِiʾْlِl", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "صَٰدِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِdَٰṣ", "root": "صدق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَعْلَمُ غَيْبَ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِۚ وَٱللَّهُ بَصِيرٌۢ بِمَا تَعْمَلُونَ ", "translation": "Indeed, Allāh knows the unseen [aspects] of the heavens and the earth. And Allāh is Seeing of what you do.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "غَيْبَ", "translit": "َbْyَغ", "root": "غيب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بَصِيرٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢryِṣَb", "root": "بصر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تَعْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْʿعَt", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "2": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "3": [{"arabic": "تعالى أولئك الذين امتحن الله قلوبهم للتقوى قيل نزع منها محبة الشهوات وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم المؤمن بين خمس شدائد مؤمن يحسده ومنافق يبغضه وكافر يقاتله وشيطان يضله ونفس تنازعه (1) فبين أن النفس عدو منازع يجب عليه مجاهدتها ويروى أن الله تعالى أوحى إلى داود عليه السلام يا داود حذر وأنذر أصحابك أكل الشهوات فإن القلوب المتعلقة بشهوات الدنيا عقولها عني محجوبة وقال عيسى عليه السلام طوبى لمن ترك شهوة حاضرة لموعود غائب لم يره وقال نبينا صلى الله عليه وسلم لقوم قدموا من الجهاد مرحبا بكم", "english": "But if you are incapable of that, then it behooves you not to let slip the affirmation of truth (taṣdīq) and faith (īmān) by way of receptive acceptance (talqqī) and emulation (taqlīd) of one who deserves to be emulated. For faith has a degree, just as knowledge has a degree; and knowledge is attained after faith, and it lies beyond it. God, the Exalted, said: {«يرفع الله الذين آمنوا منكم والذين أوتوا العلم درجات»} — \"God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, by degrees.\" So whoever affirms as true that opposing the desires (mukhālafat al-shahawāt) is the road to God, the Mighty and Majestic, yet has not been apprised of its cause and its secret — he is among those who believe. And when he is apprised of what we have mentioned concerning the helpers of the desires, he is among those who have been given knowledge. And to both has God promised the goodly reward.", "book_title": "Breaking the Two Desires", "section_title_ar": "Section 627", "section_title_en": "Section 627 — The Path of Opposition to the Desires: On the Consensus of the Scholars that There Is No Road to the Happiness of the Hereafter Save through Restraining the Soul from Caprice", "section_index": 627, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, spirit; that which breathes; the animating principle; also blood, and the \"evil eye\" (nafs sā'ilah)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس: الروح — the soul as the spiritual reality; in the verse {«ويحذركم الله نفسه»} it denotes the very Essence (al-dhāt); the soul is that which must be disciplined and opposed in its caprice (hawā)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self that desires and inclines; Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه\" — he poured out his blood/life; the soul as the internal adversary that must be fought (jihād al-nafs)\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; to turn over, to transform, to fluctuate; the heart as that which is constantly turning between states\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and fluctuation between conditions; the seat of spiritual perception and disease\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to turn something over; Metaphorical: the heart as the organ of spiritual understanding that can be veiled (maḥjūbah) from God by attachment to desires\n- Root: دنا (d-n-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be near, proximity; the world (al-dunyā) as that which is nearer/lower\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الدنو: القرب بالذات أو بالحكم — proximity by essence or by judgment; the world as the nearer realm that veils from the distant (al-ākhirah)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: closeness; Metaphorical: the lower world as that which is \"near\" and thus preoccupies the heart from what is higher\n- Root: أكل (a-k-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To eat, consume; also to possess and enjoy\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Eating as the most fundamental desire; the restriction of food (qillat al-ṭa'ām) as the first pillar of spiritual discipline (riyāḍah)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: consumption of food; Metaphorical: \"أكل الشهوات\" — the devouring of desires as that which veils the intellect from God\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic sentence structure in which the author interposes explanatory clauses between the subject and its predicate, as in \"فإنه لو تمتع بشيء منه أنس به وألفه\" where the conditional clause governs the entire following apodosis, requiring careful tracking of the protasis-apodosis relationship through extended digressions.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Breaking the Two Desires'", "badge": "Insight"}], "6": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا إن جاءكم فاسق بنبأ فتبينوا أن تصيبوا قوما بجهالة فلا يجوز تصديق إبليس وإن كان ثم مخيلة تدل على فساد واحتمل خلافه لم يجز أن تصدق به لأن الفاسق يتصور أن بصدق في خيره ولكن لا يجوز لك أن تصدق به حتى إن من استنكه فوجد منه رائحة الخمر لا يجوز أن يحد إذ يقال يمكن أن يكون قد تمضمض بالخمر ومجها وما شربها أو حمل عليه قهرا فكل ذلك لا محالة دلالة محتملة فلا يجوز تصديقها بالقلب", "english": "For God Most High has said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا اجتنبوا كثيرا من الظن إن بعض الظن إثم»} — \"O you who believe, avoid much suspicion; indeed, some suspicion is sin.\" The reason for its prohibition is that the secrets of hearts are known only to the Knower of the Unseen (ʿAllām al-Ghuyūb). You have no right to believe evil of another unless it is disclosed to you with certain sight (ʿiyān) that admits no interpretation. At that point you cannot but believe what you have known and witnessed. But what you have not witnessed with your eye nor heard with your ear, and then falls into your heart — it is Satan who casts it to you, and you ought to deny him, for he is the most wicked of the wicked. God Most High has said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا إن جاءكم فاسق بنبأ فتبينوا أن تصيبوا قوما بجهالة»} — \"O you who believe, if a wicked one brings you news, then verify, lest you harm a people in ignorance.\" It is not permissible to believe Satan, even if there is some indication suggesting corruption. As long as the opposite remains possible, it is not permissible to believe it. Thus, even if one detects upon another the odor of wine, it is not permissible to impose the prescribed punishment (ḥadd) upon him — for it is said: perhaps he rinsed his mouth with wine and spat it out without drinking it, or he was compelled to carry it under duress. All of this is, inevitably, a possible indication, and it is not permissible to believe it in the heart.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 687", "section_title_en": "The Cure That Restrains the Tongue from Backbiting, and the Prohibition of Backbiting in the Heart", "section_index": 687, "anchors": "- Root: غيب (ghayb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: That which is hidden from the eye; absence from sensory perception.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ghaybah (backbiting) is rooted in speaking of one who is absent (ghāʾib) — the unseen referent is absent from the listener's sight, yet present in the speaker's malice.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"speaking from behind the veil of absence\" (takallama bihi ʿan ẓahri al-ghayb). Metaphorical: the tongue's slander reaches the absent one as if traversing a hidden realm.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self; the vital spirit; that which breathes and desires.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The nafs is the locus of blameworthy traits (hawā) and the battlefield between angelic and satanic promptings; its thoughts (khaṭarāt) are distinct from settled convictions (ʿaqd al-qalb).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"self-speaking\" (ḥadīth al-nafs) as internal discourse. Metaphorical: the nafs \"inclines\" (tamīlu) toward suspicion — a leaning of the heart, not a mere passing thought.\n- Root: قلب (qalb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; the organ of understanding and intention.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Taqlīb — the heart's constant turning between states; its \"settling\" (ʿaqd) upon a judgment constitutes belief (iʿtiqād), which is the object of divine prohibition in suspicion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: turning a thing over. Metaphorical: the heart \"turns over\" a matter until it rests upon a conviction — this resting (sukūn) is what renders suspicion sinful, unlike the fleeting whisper.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and specification (bāb al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — the phrase \"ولست أعني به إلا عقد القلب\" restricts the referent of \"suspicion\" exclusively to the heart's settled judgment, excluding passing thoughts (khaṭarāt) and doubt (shakk), which are pardoned.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "9": [{"arabic": "الله فسوف نؤتيه أجرا عظيما وقال تعالى وإن طائفتان من المؤمنين اقتتلوا فأصلحوا بينهما الآية والإصلاح نهي عن البغي وإعادة إلى الطاعة فإن لم يفعل فقد أمر الله تعالى بقتاله فقال فقاتلوا التي تبغي حتى تفيء إلى أمر الله وذلك هو النهي عن المنكر وأما الأخبار فمنها ما روي عن أبي بكر الصديق رضي الله عنه أنه قال في خطبة خطبها أيها الناس إنكم تقرءون هذه الآية وتؤولونها على خلاف تأويلها يا أيها الذين آمنوا عليكم أنفسكم لا يضركم من ضل إذا اهتديتم (1) وإني سمعت رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يقول ما من قوم عملوا بالمعاصي وفيهم من يقدر أن ينكر علي", "english": "Know that the verses indicating the obligation of commanding right and forbidding wrong are numerous. Among them is His saying, may He be exalted: {«Let there arise from among you a nation inviting to good, commanding right and forbidding wrong — and those are the successful»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:104). In this verse is the declaration of obligation, for His saying, may He be exalted, \"Let there be\" (wal-takun) is a command, and the apparent meaning of a command is obligation. In it also is the declaration that success (falāḥ) is contingent upon it, for He restricted it and said: {«And those are the successful»}. In it also is the declaration that it is a collective obligation (farḍ kifāyah), not an individual obligation (farḍ ʿayn), and that when a group undertakes it, the obligation falls from the rest — for He did not say, \"Let all of you be commanders of right,\" but rather said: {«Let there arise from among you a nation»}. Thus, whenever one person or a group undertakes it, the burden falls from the others, and success is particularized to those who undertake it and directly perform it. But if all of creation were to hold back from it, the burden would encompass all those capable of it without exception.", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 521", "section_title_en": "Section 521", "section_index": 521, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "فقد أمر الله تعالى بقتاله فقال فقاتلوا التي تبغي حتى تفيء إلى أمر الله وذلك هو النهي عن المنكر وأما الأخبار فمنها ما روي عن أبي بكر الصديق رضي الله عنه أنه قال في خطبة خطبها أيها الناس إنكم تقرءون هذه الآية وتؤولونها على خلاف تأويلها يا أيها الذين آمنوا عليكم أنفسكم لا يضركم من ضل إذا اهتديتم (1) وإني سمعت رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يقول ما من قوم عملوا بالمعاصي وفيهم من يقدر أن ينكر عليهم فلم يفعل إلا يوشك أن يعمهم الله بعذاب من عنده وروى عن أبي ثعلبة الخشني أنه سأل رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم عن تفسير قوله تعال", "english": "Know that the verses indicating the obligation of commanding right and forbidding wrong are numerous. Among them is His saying, may He be exalted: {«Let there arise from among you a nation inviting to good, commanding right and forbidding wrong — and those are the successful»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:104). In this verse is the declaration of obligation, for His saying, may He be exalted, \"Let there be\" (wal-takun) is a command, and the apparent meaning of a command is obligation. In it also is the declaration that success (falāḥ) is contingent upon it, for He restricted it and said: {«And those are the successful»}. In it also is the declaration that it is a collective obligation (farḍ kifāyah), not an individual obligation (farḍ ʿayn), and that when a group undertakes it, the obligation falls from the rest — for He did not say, \"Let all of you be commanders of right,\" but rather said: {«Let there arise from among you a nation»}. Thus, whenever one person or a group undertakes it, the burden falls from the others, and success is particularized to those who undertake it and directly perform it. But if all of creation were to hold back from it, the burden would encompass all those capable of it without exception.", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 521", "section_title_en": "Section 521", "section_index": 521, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Insight"}], "11": [{"arabic": "إلا الحياة الدنيا وفي قوله تعالى ومن لم يتب فأولئك هم الظالمون إلى غير ذلك من الآيات وكان داخلا في معنى قوله عز وجل ومنهم أميون لا يعلمون الكتاب إلا أماني يعني التلاوة المجردة وقوله عز وجل وكأين من آية في السموات والأرض يمرون عليها وهم عنها معرضون لأن القرآن هو المبين لتلك الآيات في السموات والأرض ومهما تجاوزها ولم يتأثر بها كان معرضا عنها ولذلك قيل إن من لم يكن متصفا بأخلاق القرآن فإذا قرأ القرآن ناداه الله تعالى مالك ولكلامي وأنت معرض عني دع عنك كلامي إن لم تتب إلي ومثال العاصي إذا قرأ القرآن وكرره مثال", "english": "And at the mention of God, His attributes, and His names, he bows low in humility before His majesty and in apprehension of His greatness. And at the mention of the disbelievers' sayings — those things impossible for God, Mighty and Majestic, such as their attributing to Him a son or a consort — he lowers his voice and inwardly recoils in shame at the ugliness of their speech. And at the description of Paradise, his innermost being surges forth with longing for it. And at the description of the Fire, his limbs tremble in fear of it. For when the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said to Ibn Mas'ud, \"Recite to me,\" he said: \"I began reciting Surah al-Nisa'. When I reached {فكيف إذا جئنا من كل أمة بشهيد وجئنا بك على هؤلاء شهيدا} [How then shall it be when We bring from every nation a witness, and We bring you as a witness against these?] (4:41), I saw his two eyes overflowing with tears. He then said to me: 'That is enough for you now.'\" And this was because the witnessing of that very state had wholly overwhelmed his heart.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 230", "section_title_en": "The States of the Heart During Qur'anic Recitation — The Condition of the True Recieter", "section_index": 230, "anchors": "- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of trembling fear, trepidation of the heart\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the inward apprehension of fear; cited in {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a man fearful, a people fearful; the state of the heart's trembling before divine majesty\n- Root: وصف (w-ṣ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Describing a thing by its attributes and qualities\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوصف: ذكر الشيء بحليته ونعته\" — description is the mention of a thing by its adornment and attribute; may be true or false\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical: \"وجهها يصف الحسن\" — metaphorical usage where the face itself \"describes\" beauty; likewise, the tongue may \"describe\" falsehood, as in {ولا تقولوا لما تصف ألسنتكم الكذب}\n- Root: وفي (w-f-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completeness, fulfillment, reaching full measure\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — that which attains completion; \"أوفى: إذا تمم العهد ولم ينقض حفظه\" — fulfilled the covenant without breach\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" — a full-weight dirham; Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة إذا زاد عليها\" — surpassed the hundred\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The separation between the governing preposition and its object by intervening clauses, as in \"فلما بلغت {فكيف إذا جئنا...}\" where the temporal clause governs the narrative sequence", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وهذا محرم مهما كان مؤذيا كما قال تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا لا يسخر قوم من قوم عسى أن يكونوا خيرا منهم ولا نساء من نساء عسى أن يكن خيرا منهن ومعنى السخرية الاستهانة والتحقير والتنبيه على العيوب والنقائص على وجه يضحك منه وقد يكون ذلك بالمحاكاة في الفعل والقول وقد يكون بالإشارة والإيماء وإذا كان بحضرة المستهزأ به لم يسم ذلك غيبة وفيه معنى الغيبة قالت عائشة رضي الله عنها حاكيت إنسانا فقال لي النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم والله ما أحب أني حاكيت إنسانا ولي كذا وكذا (2) وقال ابن عباس في قوله تعالى يا ويلتنا ما لهذا الكتاب لا يغادر صغيرة ولا كبيرة إلا أحصاها إن الصغيرة التبسم بالاستهزاء بالمؤمن", "english": "This is forbidden whenever it is harmful, just as God—Exalted is He—has said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا لا يسخر قوم من قوم عسى أن يكونوا خيرا منهم ولا نساء من نساء عسى أن يكن خيرا منهن»} [\"O you who believe, let not a people deride a people; perhaps they may be better than them. Nor let women deride women; perhaps they may be better than them\"] (Q. 49:11). The meaning of mockery (al-sukhriyyah) is contempt, belittlement, and drawing attention to faults and defects in a manner that causes laughter at the person. This may occur through imitation in action and speech, and it may occur through gesture and indication. And if it occurs in the presence of the one being mocked, it is not called backbiting (ghībah), yet it contains the meaning of backbiting.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 673", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 673: The Eleventh Affliction: Mockery and Derision", "section_index": 673, "anchors": "- Root: سخر (s-kh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of subjugation and forced employment; also derision and making a mockery of someone.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التسخير: سياقة إلى الغرض المختص قهرا\" — driving toward a specific purpose by compulsion. The theological nuance here shifts to the passive form (سُخِرَ) and the verbal noun (سخرية) denoting the act of holding another in contempt and compelling them into a position of ridicule.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (Literal): \"فلان سخرة سخرة: يضحك منه الناس\" — one whom people laugh at. Majaz (Metaphorical): The root's use for subjugated service (as in \"سخر الله لك\") is distinct from this interpersonal derision, which is the literal sense intended here.\n- Root: ضحك (ḍ-ḥ-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Physical laughter with the baring of teeth; the molars are called الضواحك.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الضحك: انبساط الوجه وتكشر الأسنان من سرور النفس\" — expansion of the face and baring of teeth from delight of the soul. He explicitly notes: \"واستعير الضحك للسخرية\" — laughter is metaphorically extended to mockery, as in \"ضحكت منه\" (I laughed at him). This metaphorical extension is precisely the mechanism of the sin discussed here.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"افتر عن ضاحكته\" — smiling broadly. Majaz: \"ضحكت الأرض عن النبات\" — the earth \"laughs\" forth vegetation. The distinction is critical: the physical act of laughing (haqiqah) becomes blameworthy mockery (majaz) when directed at another's defects.\n- Root: هلم (h-l-m)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"هلم دعاء إلى الشيء\" — a call toward something. Derived from \"ها لم\" (come, repair to) or \"هل أم\" (is there intent?). It is an imperative summons.\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"لأَن الهاء زائدة، وأَصله هالُمّ\" — the ha is augmentative; the root is l-m-m (to repair, to mend).\n- Root: وقد (w-q-d)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"وقدت النار تقد وقودا ووقدا\" — the fire blazed. The verbal noun وقود refers both to fuel and to the resulting flame.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"وقدت النار وقودا ووقدا، واتقدت وتوقدت\" — the fire ignited and blazed. Majaz: \"قلب وقاد\" — a blazing heart (for intelligence). The imagery of a door opening to the scorner, who approaches \"بكربه وغمه\" (with his distress and grief), evokes this root's sense of burning anguish.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The prohibition is restricted by the conditional clause \"مهما كان مؤذيا\" (whenever it is harmful), and the focus is sharpened by the Quranic address \"يا أيها الذين آمنوا\" which restricts the address to believers, making the prohibition binding upon them specifically.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "12": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "ومن أبوابه سوء الظن بالمسلمين قال الله تعالى {يا أيها الذين آمنوا اجتنبوا كثيرا من الظن إن بعض الظن إثم} فمن يحكم بشر على غيره بالظن بعثه الشيطان على أن يطول فيه اللسان بالغيبة فيهلك أو يقصر في القيام بحقوقه أو يتوانى في إكرامه وينظر إليه بعين الاحتقار ويرى نفسه خيرا منه وكل ذلك من المهلكات ولأجل ذلك منع الشرع من التعرض للتهم فقال صلى الله عليه وسلم اتقوا مواضع التهم (2) حديث صفية بنت حيى أن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم كان معتكفا فأتيته فتحدثت عنده الحديث وفيه أن الشطيان يجري من ابن آدم مجرى الدم متفق عليه فانظر كيف أشفق صلى الله عليه وسلم على دينهما فحرسهما", "english": "Thābit al-Bunānī said: When the Messenger of God — peace be upon him — was sent forth, Iblīs said to his devils: \"A new event has occurred — go and see what it is.\" So they set out until they were exhausted, then came back and said: \"We do not know.\" He said: \"I shall bring you the news myself.\" So he went, then returned and said: \"God has sent Muḥammad — peace be upon him.\" He said: \"And he began dispatching his devils to the Companions of the Prophet — peace be upon him — and they would return disappointed, saying: 'We have never accompanied a people like these; we gain nothing from them — for when we reach them, they rise to their prayers, and that erases whatever we achieved.' So Iblīs said to them: 'Be patient with them — perhaps God will open the world to them, and then we shall obtain our need from them.'\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 602", "section_title_en": "On the Devil's Entrances: Wealth, Partisanship, and the Corruption of Hearts", "section_index": 602, "anchors": "- Root: شيط (shayṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of burning, scorching, or spoiling by fire; also one who is destroyed or whose blood is shed in vain.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The Shayṭān is that which is far removed from good and rebellious among jinn and men.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: meat that is burnt and spoiled. Metaphorical: one whose blood is shed in vain (shāṭa damuhu), or one who is torn apart and divided like sacrificial meat (asḥāṭū laḥm al-jazūr).\n- Root: حدث (ḥadath)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: New occurrence, event, or innovation; that which comes into being after not having been.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ḥudūth is the coming-to-be of a thing after it was not — whether accident or substance. Iḥdāth is bringing it into existence. The Prophet's advent was a \"new event\" (ḥadath).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: recent, newly occurred. Metaphorical: \"Umar was muḥaddath — one whose intuition is true, as if he were informed (yuḥaddath) of hidden matters.\n- Root: عصب (ʿaṣab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To bind, tie firmly; partisanship (taʿaṣṣub) — the binding of oneself to a group or faction.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): ʿAṣabiyyah — zealous partisanship for one's group, whether in truth or falsehood.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: binding a limb with a bandage. Metaphorical: binding oneself to a party or faction in blind allegiance.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Specification (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd) — distinguishing genuine claims from false ones through predicate focus and contrastive emphasis.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "شبهه الله تعالى بأكل لحوم الميتة فقال {أيحب أحدكم أن يأكل لحم أخيه ميتا} والملك الذي يمثله في المنام ما تطالعه الروح من اللوح المحفوظ بالأمثلة المحسوسة يمثل الغيبة بأكل لحوم الميتة حتى أن من يرى أنه يأكل لحم ميتة فإنه يغتاب الناس لأن ذلك الملك في تمثيله يراعي المشاركة والمناسبة بين الشيء وبين مثاله في المعنى الذي يجري من المثال مجرى الروح لا في ظاهر الصور فإذن حماية الأخوة بدفع ذم الأعداء وتعنت المتعنتين واجب في عقد الأخوة وقد قال مجاهد لا تذكر أخاك في غيبته إلا كما تحب أن يذكرك في غيبتك فإذن لك فيه معياران أحدهما أن تقدر", "english": "The meaning of brotherhood is thus participation (musāhamah) in prosperity and adversity. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: {«إذا أحب أحدكم أخاه فليخبره»} (When one of you loves his brother, let him inform him). (1) He commanded the informing only because it necessitates an increase of love. For if he knows that you love him, he will love you by nature, inevitably. And when you know that he also loves you, your love increases, inevitably. Thus love continues to increase from both sides and to multiply.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 422", "section_title_en": "On the Duties of Brotherhood: Speech, Praise, Defense, and Sincere Counsel (Section 422 of the Revival of the Religious Sciences)", "section_index": 422, "anchors": "- Root: نصح (naṣaḥa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of sincerity, purity, and the opposite of deceit; also \"to sew\" (naṣaḥa al-jild), indicating a mending of what is torn.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النصح: تحري فعل أو قول فيه صلاح صاحبه\" — Sincere counsel is the seeking of an act or saying in which lies the well-being of the other. It is the purification of one's intent (ikhlāṣ) toward the brother.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"نصحته ونصحت له نصحا ونصيحة\" — to advise sincerely. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"هو ناصح الجيب\" (pure of pocket/garment) and \"نصح الغيث البلاد\" (the rain fulfilled the land), indicating that true counsel is a complete and sincere pouring forth that benefits the recipient.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to distinguish the precise nature of acts (e.g., \"إنما أمر بالإخبار\" restricts the command's purpose to a specific cause: the increase of love).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "ولا تجسسوا ولا يغتب بعضكم بعضا وكم زاهد جاهل يوحش القلوب في التفتيش ويتكلم الكلام الخشن المؤذي وإنما يحسن الشيطان ذلك عنده طلبا للشهرة بأكل الحلال ولو كان باعثه محض الدين لكان خوفه على قلب مسلم أن يتأذى أشد من خوفه على بطنه أن يدخله ما لا يدري وهو غير مؤاخذ بما لا يدري إذ لم يكن ثم علامة توجب الاجتناب فليعلم أن طريق الورع الترك دون التجسس وإذا لم يكن بد من الأكل فالورع الأكل وإحسان الظن هذا هو المألوف من الصحابة رضي الله عنهم ومن زاد عليهم في الورع فهو ضال مبتدع وليس بمتبع فلن يبل", "english": "They said: \"It is scrupulousness (al-waraʿ).\" But Ḥassān ibn Abī Sinān said to them: \"Nothing is easier for me than scrupulousness — whenever anything stirs unease in my breast, I simply leave it.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 366", "section_title_en": "The Ruling on the Unknown Provider — Between Scrupulousness (Waraʿ) and Suspicion", "section_index": 366, "anchors": "- Root: ورع (w-r-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of restraint, abstaining, and holding back from what is doubtful or forbidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Waraʿ is the virtue of the God-fearing — abstaining not only from the unlawful but from the ambiguous (shubhah), as a safeguard for religion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage denotes physical withdrawal; metaphorical usage denotes moral abstention — the heart's recoil from what is suspect.\n- Root: ظن (ẓ-n-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Uncertainty, conjecture, or opinion that falls short of certainty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ẓann is divided into blameworthy suspicion (sūʾ al-ẓann) against the innocent — which is prohibited — and praiseworthy presumption of good (ḥusn al-ẓann) toward a Muslim, which is obligated by brotherhood in faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to hold an opinion. Metaphorical: the heart's inclination toward one of two possibilities without decisive evidence.\n- Root: ريبة (r-y-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Doubt, unease, or suspicion arising from a tangible indicator — not mere whim.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Rayb is the disturbance of certainty by an ambiguous circumstance; it is the legitimate trigger for inquiry, unlike baseless suspicion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: uncertainty. Metaphorical: the inward disquiet that prompts a man to investigate before acting.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The conditional and exceptive structures here (e.g., \"لا يلزمك السؤال\" and \"إلا عن ريبة\") restrict the obligation of inquiry to the presence of a genuine ground for doubt, not to mere possibility.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "عليه السلام واحدة قال الله تعالى اجتنبوا كثيرا من الظن إن بعض الظن إثم ولا تجسسوا ولا يغتب بعضكم بعضا وكم زاهد جاهل يوحش القلوب في التفتيش ويتكلم الكلام الخشن المؤذي وإنما يحسن الشيطان ذلك عنده طلبا للشهرة بأكل الحلال ولو كان باعثه محض الدين لكان خوفه على قلب مسلم أن يتأذى أشد من خوفه على بطنه أن يدخله ما لا يدري وهو غير مؤاخذ بما لا يدري إذ لم يكن ثم علامة توجب الاجتناب فليعلم أن طريق الورع الترك دون التجسس وإذا لم يكن بد من الأكل فالورع الأكل وإحسان الظن هذا هو المألوف من الصحابة رضي الله عنهم ومن زاد عليهم في الورع فهو", "english": "They said: \"It is scrupulousness (al-waraʿ).\" But Ḥassān ibn Abī Sinān said to them: \"Nothing is easier for me than scrupulousness — whenever anything stirs unease in my breast, I simply leave it.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 366", "section_title_en": "The Ruling on the Unknown Provider — Between Scrupulousness (Waraʿ) and Suspicion", "section_index": 366, "anchors": "- Root: ورع (w-r-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of restraint, abstaining, and holding back from what is doubtful or forbidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Waraʿ is the virtue of the God-fearing — abstaining not only from the unlawful but from the ambiguous (shubhah), as a safeguard for religion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage denotes physical withdrawal; metaphorical usage denotes moral abstention — the heart's recoil from what is suspect.\n- Root: ظن (ẓ-n-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Uncertainty, conjecture, or opinion that falls short of certainty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ẓann is divided into blameworthy suspicion (sūʾ al-ẓann) against the innocent — which is prohibited — and praiseworthy presumption of good (ḥusn al-ẓann) toward a Muslim, which is obligated by brotherhood in faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to hold an opinion. Metaphorical: the heart's inclination toward one of two possibilities without decisive evidence.\n- Root: ريبة (r-y-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Doubt, unease, or suspicion arising from a tangible indicator — not mere whim.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Rayb is the disturbance of certainty by an ambiguous circumstance; it is the legitimate trigger for inquiry, unlike baseless suspicion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: uncertainty. Metaphorical: the inward disquiet that prompts a man to investigate before acting.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The conditional and exceptive structures here (e.g., \"لا يلزمك السؤال\" and \"إلا عن ريبة\") restrict the obligation of inquiry to the presence of a genuine ground for doubt, not to mere possibility.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Insight"}], "13": [{"arabic": "من الخنازير ولذلك قال تعالى {يا أيها الناس إنا خلقناكم من ذكر وأنثى} أي لا تفاوت في أنسابكم لاجتماعكم في أصل واحد ثم ذكر فائدة النسب فقال {وجعلناكم شعوبا وقبائل لتعارفوا} ثم بين أن الشرف بالتقوى لا بالنسب فقال {إن أكرمكم عند الله أتقاكم} ولما قيل لرسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم من أكرم الناس من أكيس الناس لم يقل من ينتمي إلى نسبي ولكن قال أكرمهم أكثرهم للموت ذكرا وأشدهم له استعدادا (1) حديث إن الله قد أذهب عنكم عيبة الجاهلية الحديث أخرجه أبو داود والترمذي وحسنه من حديث أبي هريرة ورواه الترمذي أيضا من حديث ابن عمر وقال غريب", "english": "The fourth: pride in noble lineage, like the pride of the Hashimite, to the point that some of them imagine that they will be saved by the nobility of their lineage and the salvation of their forefathers, and that they are forgiven; and some of them fancy that all of creation are their clients and slaves. Its remedy is that he should know that whenever he contradicts his forefathers in their deeds and their moral traits, yet imagines himself to be joined with them, he has indeed been ignorant. And if he emulates his forefathers, then their moral traits were not pride, but rather fear, self-contempt, magnifying others, and self-blame. For they were ennobled by obedience, knowledge, and praiseworthy qualities — not by lineage. So let him seek honor through that by which they were honored. For those who shared their lineage and participated with them in their tribes, yet did not believe in God and the Last Day, were in God's sight worse than dogs and viler than swine. For this reason God (Exalted is He) said: {«يا أيها الناس إنا خلقناكم من ذكر وأنثى»} — \"O mankind, indeed We have created you from a male and a female\" — that is, there is no disparity in your lineages because you all share a single origin. Then He mentioned the benefit of lineage, saying: {«وجعلناكم شعوبا وقبائل لتعارفوا»} — \"And We made you peoples and tribes that you may know one another.\" Then He clarified that honor is by piety, not by lineage, saying: {«إن أكرمكم عند الله أتقاكم»} — \"Indeed, the most noble of you in the sight of God is the most righteous of you.\" And when it was said to the Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him), \"Who is the most noble of people?\" — he did not say, \"Whoever traces himself to my lineage,\" but rather he said, \"The most noble of them is the one most mindful of death and the most prepared for it.\" (1) A hadith: \"Indeed, God has removed from you the pride of the Age of Ignorance\" — this hadith was transmitted by Abu Dawud and al-Tirmidhi, who deemed it sound (ḥasan), from the narration of Abu Hurayrah; al-Tirmidhi also narrated it from the narration of Ibn ʿUmar and said it is gharīb (uncommon).", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 881", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 881: On the Wonders of Pride in Strength, Intellect, and Lineage, and Their Remedies", "section_index": 881, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿaql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The faculty of restraint and comprehension; the intellect as that which binds one from error.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — the power prepared for receiving knowledge, and the knowledge acquired thereby.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sound judgment after folly; Metaphorical: \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار\" — a palm that does not accept pollination, i.e., lacks receptivity.\n- Root: نسب (nasab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Lineage and kinship through forebears.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النسب والنسبة: اشتراك من جهة أحد الأبوين\" — participation through one of the two parents; two types: vertical (through ancestors) and horizontal (through collaterals).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فلان حسيب نسيب\" — noble in deed and lineage; Metaphorical: \"بين الشيئين مناسبة\" — proportionality or correspondence between things.\n- Root: حدث (ḥadath)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; newness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — a thing's coming to be after it was not; \"المحدث: ما أوجد بعد أن لم يكن\" — the originated.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: recent events; Metaphorical: \"كان عمر رضي الله عنه محدثا\" — one inspired with true discernment.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which animates.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the spirit; also the very essence (dhāt) when attributed to God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: preciousness; Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه\" — poured out his blood, i.e., his life.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition (الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the permissible separation between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase, applied here to parse the conditional and relative clauses conveying causal relations between pride and its consequences.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "{وجعلناكم شعوبا وقبائل لتعارفوا} ثم بين أن الشرف بالتقوى لا بالنسب فقال {إن أكرمكم عند الله أتقاكم} ولما قيل لرسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم من أكرم الناس من أكيس الناس لم يقل من ينتمي إلى نسبي ولكن قال أكرمهم أكثرهم للموت ذكرا وأشدهم له استعدادا (1) حديث إن الله قد أذهب عنكم عيبة الجاهلية الحديث أخرجه أبو داود والترمذي وحسنه من حديث أبي هريرة ورواه الترمذي أيضا من حديث ابن عمر وقال غريب وقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم يا معشر قريش لا تأتي الناس بالأعمال يوم القيامة وتأتون بالدنيا تحملونها", "english": "The fourth: pride in noble lineage, like the pride of the Hashimite, to the point that some of them imagine that they will be saved by the nobility of their lineage and the salvation of their forefathers, and that they are forgiven; and some of them fancy that all of creation are their clients and slaves. Its remedy is that he should know that whenever he contradicts his forefathers in their deeds and their moral traits, yet imagines himself to be joined with them, he has indeed been ignorant. And if he emulates his forefathers, then their moral traits were not pride, but rather fear, self-contempt, magnifying others, and self-blame. For they were ennobled by obedience, knowledge, and praiseworthy qualities — not by lineage. So let him seek honor through that by which they were honored. For those who shared their lineage and participated with them in their tribes, yet did not believe in God and the Last Day, were in God's sight worse than dogs and viler than swine. For this reason God (Exalted is He) said: {«يا أيها الناس إنا خلقناكم من ذكر وأنثى»} — \"O mankind, indeed We have created you from a male and a female\" — that is, there is no disparity in your lineages because you all share a single origin. Then He mentioned the benefit of lineage, saying: {«وجعلناكم شعوبا وقبائل لتعارفوا»} — \"And We made you peoples and tribes that you may know one another.\" Then He clarified that honor is by piety, not by lineage, saying: {«إن أكرمكم عند الله أتقاكم»} — \"Indeed, the most noble of you in the sight of God is the most righteous of you.\" And when it was said to the Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him), \"Who is the most noble of people?\" — he did not say, \"Whoever traces himself to my lineage,\" but rather he said, \"The most noble of them is the one most mindful of death and the most prepared for it.\" (1) A hadith: \"Indeed, God has removed from you the pride of the Age of Ignorance\" — this hadith was transmitted by Abu Dawud and al-Tirmidhi, who deemed it sound (ḥasan), from the narration of Abu Hurayrah; al-Tirmidhi also narrated it from the narration of Ibn ʿUmar and said it is gharīb (uncommon).", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 881", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 881: On the Wonders of Pride in Strength, Intellect, and Lineage, and Their Remedies", "section_index": 881, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿaql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The faculty of restraint and comprehension; the intellect as that which binds one from error.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — the power prepared for receiving knowledge, and the knowledge acquired thereby.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sound judgment after folly; Metaphorical: \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار\" — a palm that does not accept pollination, i.e., lacks receptivity.\n- Root: نسب (nasab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Lineage and kinship through forebears.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النسب والنسبة: اشتراك من جهة أحد الأبوين\" — participation through one of the two parents; two types: vertical (through ancestors) and horizontal (through collaterals).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فلان حسيب نسيب\" — noble in deed and lineage; Metaphorical: \"بين الشيئين مناسبة\" — proportionality or correspondence between things.\n- Root: حدث (ḥadath)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; newness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — a thing's coming to be after it was not; \"المحدث: ما أوجد بعد أن لم يكن\" — the originated.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: recent events; Metaphorical: \"كان عمر رضي الله عنه محدثا\" — one inspired with true discernment.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which animates.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the spirit; also the very essence (dhāt) when attributed to God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: preciousness; Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه\" — poured out his blood, i.e., his life.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition (الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the permissible separation between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase, applied here to parse the conditional and relative clauses conveying causal relations between pride and its consequences.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "الحسب والشرف والنسب فنظرت فيها فإذا هي لا شيء ثم نظرت إلى قول الله تعالى إن أكرمكم عند الله أتقاكم فعملت في التقوى حتى أكون عند الله كريما الخامسة أني نظرت إلى هذا الخلق وهم يطعن بعضهم في بعض ويلعن بعضهم بعضا وأصل هذا كله الحسد ثم نظرت إلى قول الله عز وجل نحن قسمنا بينهم معيشتهم في الحياة الدنيا فتركت الحسد واجتنبت الخلق وعلمت أن القسمة من عند الله سبحانه وتعالى فتركت عداوة الخلق عني السادسة نظرت إلى هذا الخلق يبغي بعضهم على بعض ويقاتل بعضهم بعضا فرجعت إلى قول الله عز وجل إن الشيطان لكم عدو فاتخذوه عدوا فعاديته وحده واجتهدت في أخذ حذري منه", "english": "Know that the scholar who does not act upon his knowledge is condemned by the Book, the Sunnah, and the sayings of the sages. God, exalted be He, said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا لم تقولون ما لا تفعلون * كبر مقتا عند الله أن تقولوا ما لا تفعلون»} (\"O you who believe, why do you say what you do not do? Most hateful it is to God that you say what you do not do\"). And He said: {«أتأمرون الناس بالبر وتنسون أنفسكم»} (\"Do you command people to righteousness while you forget yourselves?\"). And He said: {«ومن الناس من يعجبك قوله في الحياة الدنيا ويشهد الله على ما في قلبه وهو ألد الخصام»} (\"And among people is he whose speech pleases you in the worldly life, and he calls God to witness what is in his heart, yet he is the fiercest of opponents\").", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 54", "section_title_en": "Section 54", "section_index": 54, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "14": [{"arabic": "وأما الاختلاف فقوله تعالى {قالت الأعراب آمنا قل لم تؤمنوا ولكن قولوا أسلمنا} ومعناه استسلمنا في الظاهر فأراد بالإيمان ههنا التصديق بالقلب فقط وبالإسلام الاستسلام ظاهرا باللسان والجوارح وفي حديث جبرائيل عليه السلام لما سأله عن الإيمان فقال أن تؤمن بالله وملائكته وكتبه ورسله واليوم الآخر وبالبعث بعد الموت وبالحساب وبالقدر خيره وشره فقال فما الإسلام فأجاب بذكر الخصال الخمس (3) فعبر بالإسلام عن تسليم الظاهر بالقول والعمل وفي الحديث عن سعد أنه صلى الله عليه وسلم أعطى رجلا عطاء ولم يعط الآخر فقال له سعد يا رسول الله تركت فلانا لم", "english": "The truth in this matter is that faith (iman) is an expression for assent (tasdiq). God, exalted be He, has said: {وَمَا أَنتَ بِمُؤْمِنٍ لِّنَا} [And you are not one to believe us] (Quran 12:17) — that is, not one to assent to us. And Islam is an expression for surrender (taslim) and self-abandonment (istislam) through acquiescence (idhan) and compliance (inqiyad), and the abandonment of recalcitrance, refusal, obstinacy, and stubbornness.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 98", "section_title_en": "On Whether Islam and Faith (Iman) Are Identical or Distinct", "section_index": 98, "anchors": "- Root: أمن (amana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes safety, security, and freedom from fear; the isti'man form yields 'belief' as an act of granting credence and trust.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإيمان هو التصديق\" — Faith (iman) is fundamentally assent (tasdiq), a conviction of the heart, not a mere verbal profession.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage ties iman to tasdiq (verification/assent); metaphorical extension applies it to outward compliance only in a derived sense.\n- Root: سلم (salima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes soundness, safety, and freedom from defect; the istislam form yields 'submission' as an act of yielding and surrendering.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإسلام عبارة عن التسليم والاستسلام\" — Islam is surrender (taslim) and self-abandonment (istislam) through compliance and obedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage encompasses outward acts of the limbs; metaphorical (Majaz) usage can extend to inward assent, but the primary domain is the external.\n- Root: صدق (sadaqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes truthfulness and veracity in speech and report.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول\" — Truth and falsehood originate in speech; tasdiq is the act of affirming a report as true.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is in verbal confirmation; metaphorical extension applies to 'true in resolve' or 'true in attack,' i.e., confirming through action.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — Governing the permissible separation between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase, relevant here for parsing the conditional and contrastive structures in the theological arguments.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "15": [{"arabic": "فقد قال الله تعالى إنما المؤمنون الذين آمنوا بالله ورسوله ثم لم يرتابوا وجاهدوا بأموالهم وأنفسهم في سبيل الله أولئك هم الصادقون {فيكون}", "english": "If you say: \"What is the meaning of the Salaf's statement, 'I am a believer, if God wills' — given that the exception (istithnā') implies doubt, and doubt in faith is unbelief? Yet they all refrained from giving an unqualified answer affirming faith and were cautious about it.\"", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 102", "section_title_en": "On the Meaning of the Salaf's Statement \"I am a Believer, If God Wills\" — Section 102", "section_index": 102, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, the soul; that which is breathed; the innermost reality of a person.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس is used for the rūḥ (spirit), for the dhāt (essence/self), and for the seat of intention and knowledge. When one says \"he knows in himself (nafsihi) that he is a believer,\" it denotes certitude residing in the very core of one's being, not a corporeal faculty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage refers to the soul's reflexive self-awareness; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to \"blood\" (dam) or \"the eye\" (ʿayn) of envy. Here, \"in himself\" (fī nafsihi) is literal self-knowledge, not mere outward profession.\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Face, countenance; the foremost part; a mode, aspect, or direction.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The face is the most noble apparent part of a thing; hence it is used for the \"aspect\" or \"manner\" (wajh) of an affair. \"Four aspects\" (wujūh) means four distinct modes of interpretation or rational grounds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the face of a person or object. Metaphorical: \"the face of the matter\" (wajh al-amr) — its most salient and honorable dimension. Here, the \"aspects\" are hermeneutical angles, not physical parts.\n- Root: ايم (a-y-m)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The unmarried state (aym); one who has no spouse. Not applicable here directly, but the root's semantic field of \"being alone/without attachment\" contrasts with īmān's root (a-m-n) — security and trust — which is the operative term in this text.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Not directly relevant; the operative root is أمن (a-m-n), meaning security from fear, and by extension, faith (īmān) as the state of being secure in God's truth.\n- Root: كان (k-w-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be, to exist; the copula of predication.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kāna denotes existence and becoming; in theological usage, it grounds the distinction between what is (mawjūd) and what is merely asserted. The question \"Are you a believer?\" (a-mu'min anta?) uses kāna implicitly to ask about actual, present state — not future contingency.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: existence. Metaphorical: the \"being-so\" of a predicate. The istithnā' (exception) with \"in shā' Allāh\" suspends the copula's certainty, shifting from assertive existence to contingent hope.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الاستثناء والتعليق بالمشيئة (The Chapter of Exception and Suspension by the Divine Will) — When an exception clause (istithnā') follows a positive assertion, it grammatically suspends the universality or certainty of the predication. In theological discourse, this suspension may be semantic (doubt) or pragmatic (deference, etiquette, or self-deprecation), and Sibawayh's parsing of conditional suspension (taʿlīq) shows that the apodosis need not be negated — only its absolute, unqualified force is tempered.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 400, 2865], [2, 2865, 4227], [3, 4227, 6035], [4, 6035, 6662], [5, 6662, 8603], [6, 8603, 9447], [7, 9447, 10411], [8, 10411, 11363], [9, 11363, 13051], [10, 13051, 14944], [11, 14944, 15896], [12, 15896, 16776], [13, 16776, 17728], [14, 17728, 18885], [15, 18885, 20020]], "2": [[1, 400, 3158], [2, 3158, 4682], [3, 4682, 6705], [4, 6705, 7406], [5, 7406, 10022], [6, 10022, 12153], [7, 12153, 13151], [8, 13151, 14176], [9, 14176, 16165], [10, 16165, 17271], [11, 17271, 19226], [12, 19226, 19738], [13, 19738, 21356], [14, 21356, 22408], [15, 22408, 23891], [16, 23891, 25105], [17, 25105, 25698], [18, 25698, 26885], [19, 26885, 28773], [20, 28773, 30310], [21, 30310, 31011], [22, 31011, 32873]], "3": [[1, 400, 1319], [2, 1319, 2741], [3, 2741, 4598], [4, 4598, 6562], [5, 6562, 7368], [6, 7368, 8526], [7, 8526, 9520], [8, 9520, 11433], [9, 11433, 12855], [10, 12855, 13963], [11, 13963, 14957], [12, 14957, 16619], [13, 16619, 18923], [14, 18923, 19653], [15, 19